@*
    OdsCapacityField — a capacity-limit control: a right-aligned numeric input paired with
    a "No limit" switch (Odyssey Design System · components/CapacityField, issue #343 §3).

    The control behind the count caps on the System Settings import/export groups, where a
    limit is either a finite number or explicitly unbounded. Composes OdsNumberField and
    OdsSwitch — never hand-rolled — so the "role=switch"/"aria-checked" pair MudBlazor
    already emits for OdsSwitch, and OdsNumberField's help/error/aria-describedby wiring,
    both come along for free.

    Tri-state, PAGE-OWNED draft: the caller keeps BOTH Unlimited and Value — this component
    never clears Value when Unlimited flips on. Toggling "No limit" on disables (and visually
    replaces) the numeric input with literal "No limit" text, but the last entered number is
    retained by the caller so toggling back off is not a data-losing action; the number is
    simply never sent to the server while Unlimited is true.

    Labelling: the number input is labelled by the row title (AriaLabelledBy) and described by
    the row description (AriaDescribedBy) — the hint text lives in the row, never here. The
    switch's accessible name is composed from Label (e.g. "Maximum contacts per import — no
    limit"), since it carries no visible text label of its own.
*@

<div class="@WrapperClass">
    @if (Unlimited)
    {
        @if (Inline)
        {
            <span class="odc-capacity-value">No limit</span>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="odc-capacity-nolimit">No limit</div>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <OdsNumberField Class="odc-capacity-num"
                         Value="@Value"
                         ValueChanged="@OnValueChanged"
                         Min="@Min" Max="@Max" Step="1" Align="right"
                         Disabled="@Disabled"
                         Error="@Error"
                         AriaLabelledBy="@AriaLabelledBy"
                         AriaDescribedBy="@AriaDescribedBy" />
    }
    @if (Inline)
    {
        @* The pill carries the INVERSE action, so it never repeats the words already showing as the
           value and its pressed state reads as a state rather than a stutter. aria-pressed carries
           the current state; the label carries what pressing it does — the two are deliberately
           opposite, which is why the accessible name is composed rather than left as the text. *@
        <button type="button" class="odc-capacity-pill"
                disabled="@Disabled"
                aria-pressed="@(Unlimited ? "true" : "false")"
                aria-label="@($"{Label} — no limit")"
                @onclick="@(() => OnUnlimitedChanged(!Unlimited))">
            @(Unlimited ? "Set a limit" : "No limit")
        </button>
    }
    else
    {
        <label class="@($"odc-capacity-toggle {(Disabled ? "disabled" : "")}".TrimEnd())">
            <span class="odc-capacity-toggle-lbl">No limit</span>
            <OdsSwitch Checked="@Unlimited" CheckedChanged="@OnUnlimitedChanged"
                       Disabled="@Disabled" AriaLabel="@($"{Label} — no limit")" />
        </label>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The finite limit, or null while empty. Retained (never cleared here) while
    /// <see cref="Unlimited"/> is true — the caller owns this draft value.</summary>
    [Parameter] public decimal? Value { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<decimal?> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>True = no limit. The number input is hidden (replaced by "No limit" text) but
    /// <see cref="Value"/> is never cleared by this component.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Unlimited { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> UnlimitedChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The setting's title — composes the switch's accessible name (e.g. "… — no limit").</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Id of the row title — labels the number input (<c>aria-labelledby</c>).</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AriaLabelledBy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Id of the row description/hint — describes the number input (<c>aria-describedby</c>).</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? AriaDescribedBy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Error message — shown on the number input, alongside (never instead of) the row hint.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Error { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int Min { get; set; } = 1;

    [Parameter] public int Max { get; set; } = 1_000_000;

    /// <summary>Disables both the number input and the switch (e.g. the caller lacks the write claim).</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// <see cref="OdsCapacityFieldVariant.Stacked"/> (default) is the <c>OdsSettingRow</c> form — the
    /// input over a "No limit" switch, right-aligned in the row's control column.
    /// <see cref="OdsCapacityFieldVariant.Inline"/> is the <c>OdsSettingField</c> form: one line
    /// inside the notched frame, the value then a pill at the trailing edge. There is no room inside
    /// the frame for a switch plus its own label, and no second line to put them on.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public OdsCapacityFieldVariant Variant { get; set; } = OdsCapacityFieldVariant.Stacked;

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private bool Inline => Variant == OdsCapacityFieldVariant.Inline;

    private string WrapperClass =>
        string.Join(' ', new[] { "odc-capacity", Inline ? "inline" : null, Class }
            .Where(value => !string.IsNullOrEmpty(value)));

    private Task OnValueChanged(decimal? value) => ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(value);

    private Task OnUnlimitedChanged(bool value) => UnlimitedChanged.InvokeAsync(value);
}
